    "q-81511": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81511",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Consider the vector $x$ and the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the plane given that $v⊥w.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ \nAnd, since the set $\\{v,w\\}$ is orthogonal, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ is given by \n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the orthogonal projections of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1}\\}$ and $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{2}\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot v}{v\\cdot v}v=\\frac{10\\cdot (-2)+6\\cdot 1+14\\cdot 3}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}v=2[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot w}{w\\cdot w}w=\\frac{10\\cdot 2+6\\cdot (-2)+14\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}w=3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{v}\\,x+{\\text{proj}}_{w}\\,x \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
